nt  provides the first preliminary  assemblies of nine complete human  centromeric regions  and there's one example shown here from  a figure in the paper so  again highlighting that there are micro  heterogeneities  in these highly repetitive regions and  the hi-fi data are accurate enough to  resolve those  and can be used in a de novo assembly  context with just one technology  to put that together correctly on the  other end of the chromosome of course  you have telomeres  and um this is a different paper um by a  preprint by chris mason who specifically  looked at  telomeres with these hifi data and  this preprint reports the first motif  composition maps of human telomeric  diplotypes on the multi-kilo-based scale  so of course the human genome is diploid  so you have two copies of each  chromosome  and what this figure here shows is that  the hifi data are so good  and so accurate that you can actually  differentiate  the telomeres of those two um  chromosomes from all these chromosomes  the two alleles  which is what not possible uh chris  mason also  compared on the two technologies um the  pec bio ccs that's another term for hifi  um and the proles ion and found that  there's  uh substantially better quality and  resolution  and the correct assignment and  resolution and characterization of the  telomeres compared to the  other long-range technology so in the  following i want to tell you a little  bit more about how this  how this looks in practice and um i'm  using an example of an ongoing  collaboration  and work that was really led by tina  lindsey fulton ira hall and chris  markovitz from the mcdonald team  institute from washington university  um tina gave a talk at the hbt  conference  and some of the slides  that she presented i want to share with  you here as well so this is on  a well-characterized human genome na  19240 which is a reuben an african  genome  and so they sequenced um and here's a  representative example of the sequencing  performance  on the left you see um the raw reads  um you so you see most of the data is  from polymerase reits that are known  excess of 200 000 bases and so that  allows you to generate very high quality  and  long hi-fi rates that's in the middle of  the average  insert length of the dna fragments about  18 kilobases  and on the right is the equality so the  x-axis there  is the accuracy so q20 on the left and  q30  240 and so forth and the number of reads  and so these these uh statistics graphs  are nice but they don't really reflect  very well  of what a hifi read really looks like so  what i thought i would do is  show you one and uh so what i've done  here is taken  an average or the from the from the mode  of both of these distributions you can  see that on the upper right  so i took an 18 kilobase read that has  the typical accuracy which is about q35  and i've just printed it here and so  this is the whole wreath  18 342 bases and  you can predict from the number of turns  and number of times the polymerase went  around the smart cell  what the accuracy of that lead will be  and the predicted qe is 35.  and so now we can map that read against  the denoble assembly and ask  you know what what's was that a good  prediction and what's the right answer  and we find that out of those 18  342 bases 18 336 were correct and there  were six  errors which are indicated by the red uh  little triangles and that computes to  99.967 percent accurate for this  particular height  i read qe 34.85 so that's that's right  on the money so  so you can see that uh they're  in these hi-fi reads thousands of bases  and stretches that are  perfectly accurate and uh there are only  very few errors remaining  and that really i hope visualizes a  little bit better the power  that you can use this new type of data  of long and accurate sequence rates for  the normal assembly  and so they ran for smart cells because  they wanted to over sample to see how  that  would look with regard to the c